  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05JI">Lemma <span data-tag="05JI">110.39.1</span>.</a> There exists a local homomorphism $A \to B$ of local domains which is essentially of finite type and such that $A/\mathfrak m_ A \to B/\mathfrak m_ B$ is finite such that for every prime $\mathfrak q \not= \mathfrak m_ B$ of $B$ the ring map $A \to B/\mathfrak q$ is not the localization of a quasi-finite ring map. </p>
